## Core entities

**Installation** (the main object — one canopy project at one location)
- `id`
- `title` — e.g. "IOCL Fuel Station, Whitefield"
- `slug` — for the URL
- `location` — city/region (used for filtering, and gives the site a "we're everywhere" feel)
- `client` / `brand` — the petrol company (IOCL, HPCL, Shell, etc.) — optional if he wants to name clients
- `canopy_type` — e.g. cantilever, flat-roof, curved, branded fascia — even without a catalog, this is useful as a filter tag
- `year_completed`
- `description` — short story of the project (span, materials, anything notable)
- `cover_image_id` — which photo represents this project in grid/thumbnail views
- `is_featured` — boolean, to pin standout projects to the homepage
- `status` — published / draft (so admin can prep before it goes live)
- `created_at`, `updated_at`

**Photo** (belongs to an Installation — one-to-many)
- `id`
- `installation_id` — foreign key
- `image_url`
- `caption` — optional, e.g. "Night view showing LED fascia lighting"
- `sort_order` — so admin can control gallery order per project
- `is_cover` — flag if this is the one used as thumbnail

**Tag** (optional, many-to-many with Installation)
- `id`, `name` — lets you filter by things beyond location/type, e.g. "LED branding," "large-span," "highway station" — only add this if he wants filterable gallery, otherwise skip it for simplicity

**Inquiry / Lead** (from the contact/quote form — unrelated to gallery but still needed)
- `id`, `name`, `phone`, `email`, `message`, `submitted_at`, `status` (new/contacted/closed)

**User** (admin login — single user for now)
- `id`, `email`, `password_hash`, `role` (default `"admin"`) — `role` costs nothing now and avoids a migration if staff logins are ever added
- `ponytail:` single-admin assumption is baked in; adding multi-staff later needs a role-check middleware pass

## Why structured this way

- Making **Photo** its own table (not just an array of URLs on Installation) means the admin panel can reorder photos, set captions, and pick a cover image independently — this is what makes the gallery feel curated instead of a raw dump.
- `canopy_type` and `location` as structured fields (not free text buried in the description) is what lets the public gallery have real filters — "show me curved canopies" or "show me projects in Bangalore" — without you needing a full product catalog.
- `is_featured` + `status` give the admin control over what's shown on the homepage vs. buried deeper in the full gallery, without needing to delete anything.

## Pages this supports

- **Homepage** — featured installations grid, pulling `is_featured = true`
- **Gallery/Projects page** — full grid of all published installations, filterable by `location` / `canopy_type`
- **Installation detail page** — the photo gallery for that one project + description + client + year
- **Admin: Installations list** — CRUD, toggle featured/published
- **Admin: Installation editor** — upload/reorder/caption photos, set fields
- **Admin: Leads inbox** — view/manage form submissions
